esville's average low shows no clear change: +0.05\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.03 to +0.12) that includes zero. Zanesville's average high shows no clear change: \u22120.07\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.15 to +0.02) that includes zero. July and August highs have edged down across the full record; the 1930s summers were the hottest in it. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.41\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.04 to +0.77) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.57\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.14 to +1.00). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 1 of 12 months. Highs rose in none; 4 months fell.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Zanesville's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.16 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 38.5 inches a year. The wettest year was 1990 (54.47 in); the driest, 1900 (22.55 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 5 daily record highs, 4 daily record lows"}}